var ARCHIVE=[{"id": "JAH-TRIES-000001", "title": "Trie Overview: What It Is and Why It Matters", "d": "The trie: a tree where words sharing prefixes share nodes.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nA trie (pronounced 'try,' from 'retrieval') is a tree where each edge represents a character and each node represents the prefix spelled by the path from the root. Insert 'cat,' 'car,' and 'card': they share the 'ca' path, then branch — 'cat' ends at t, 'car' at r, 'card' continues to d. A million words with common stems share their prefixes instead of repeating them.\n\nTries matter because prefix problems are everywhere: autocomplete (type 'ca' → suggest 'cat,' 'car,' 'card'), spell checking (walk the word; failure points at the typo), IP routing (longest-prefix match), word games (Boggle, Scrabble — enumerate the subtree), and dictionaries. The headline complexity: insert, search, and startsWith all cost O(m) for word length m — independent of how many words are stored. A billion-word vocabulary doesn't slow down a lookup; only the word's length matters.\n\nThe trade is memory: each node holds a children map (array or hash table), and sparse tries waste space on near-empty nodes — compression (radix trees) answers that. The trie is the purest 'tree plus one idea' in the database: the one idea is that node identity IS the path, and everything else follows.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "overview", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000002", "title": "Trie: How It Works — Core Operations", "d": "Walking characters: insert, search, startsWith, and the end-of-word flag.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nInsert(word): start at the root; for each character, follow the edge — creating the child node if missing; after the last character, mark the node as end-of-word. Search(word): walk the edges; if any is missing → absent; at the end, return the end-of-word flag ('car' is a word AND a prefix of 'card' — the flag distinguishes them). startsWith(prefix): walk like search but return True at the end regardless of the flag — reaching the node proves the prefix exists.\n\nEach operation walks exactly m edges: O(m) time, O(m) new nodes worst-case for insert (fewer when prefixes are shared), O(1) extra space for search. Deletion: unmark end-of-word; optionally prune nodes that become childless and non-terminal (post-order cleanup). Counting words with a prefix: descend to the prefix node, count terminal nodes in its subtree (O(subtree) — or O(m) with per-node counters maintained on insert). The operations are all 'walk the word' — the trie's beautiful uniformity.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "operations": [{"op": "insert", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "walk/create edges; mark end-of-word"}, {"op": "search", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "walk edges; check end-of-word flag"}, {"op": "startsWith", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "walk edges; reaching the node suffices"}, {"op": "delete", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "unmark; prune childless non-terminal nodes"}, {"op": "count prefix", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "with per-node counters; O(subtree) without"}], "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000003", "title": "Trie: Internals, Trade-offs and When to Use It", "d": "Children maps, memory hunger, and the prefix problems that justify it.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nInternally a trie node is a children map plus an end-of-word flag (plus optional counters/payloads). The children representation is the key engineering choice: fixed arrays (size = alphabet, O(1) access by char code, wasteful for sparse nodes — but perfect for binary tries and DNA's 4-letter alphabet), hash tables (memory-proportional, O(1) average, Python's natural choice), or sorted lists (compact, O(log k) per step — for tiny alphabets or frozen tries). Each choice trades memory against speed differently.\n\nThe trade-offs: tries are memory-hungry (a node per prefix character — 'antidisestablishmentarianism' costs 28 nodes for one word, though shared prefixes amortize this across vocabularies); they don't support ordered iteration or range queries (that's the BST's job); and they're overkill when a hash set would do (exact-word lookup with no prefix needs). But for prefix problems they're unmatched: autocomplete, longest-prefix match, and lexicon-constrained search have no better structure.\n\nReach for tries for prefix queries, autocomplete, spell-check, routing tables, and word games. Reach past them for exact-match-only (hash sets — less memory), ordered data (BSTs), or tiny alphabets with bit-level needs (binary tries are their own beautiful subfield). The trie's question is 'what starts with this prefix?' — when that's the question, nothing else competes.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "overview", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000004", "title": "Trie Node Anatomy", "d": "Children map plus end-of-word flag: the two fields that make a trie.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nA trie node needs exactly two things: children (mapping character → child node) and is_end (whether a word terminates here — distinguishing 'car' the word from 'car' the prefix of 'card'). Everything else is augmentation: count (words in subtree — for prefix counting and autocomplete ranking), value (payload for map-like tries: word → definition), frequency (for suggestion ranking).\n\nThe children map's representation decides the memory/speed trade (arrays for dense alphabets, hash tables for sparse, sorted lists for frozen). The root represents the empty prefix — every word's path starts there. Note what nodes DON'T store: the character itself (it's on the incoming edge, recoverable from the parent's map key) — storing it too is redundant but sometimes convenient for debugging. The anatomy is minimal; the power is in the paths.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "program": "class TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.children = {}   # char -> TrieNode\n        self.is_end = False  # a word ends here\n\nroot = TrieNode()\nroot.children['c'] = TrieNode()\nroot.children['c'].children['a'] = TrieNode()\nnode = root.children['c'].children['a']\nnode.is_end = True   # the word 'ca'... or a prefix of longer words\nprint(len(root.children))  # 1 — one edge from root", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000005", "title": "Trie Insert", "d": "Walking and creating: O(m), sharing every possible prefix.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nInsert(word): node = root; for each ch: if ch not in node.children, create it; node = node.children[ch]. After the loop: node.is_end = True. Each character costs one map operation: O(m) time. New nodes: at most m (fewer when prefixes exist — inserting 'card' after 'car' creates one node, not four). Space: O(m) worst-case new nodes.\n\nThe sharing is automatic and exact: 'car' and 'cat' share 'ca' because the walk reuses existing nodes — no deduplication pass needed. Inserting a word that's already present just re-marks is_end (idempotent). Inserting a prefix of an existing word ('car' after 'card') marks an interior node — the flag is what makes interior words work. The operation is so simple it's almost not worth optimizing; the engineering lives in the children-map representation, not the walk.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "insert", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "walk/create per character; mark end-of-word"}], "program": "class Trie:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = {}\n    def insert(self, word):\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in word:\n            node = node.setdefault(ch, {})\n        node['#'] = True   # end-of-word marker\n\nt = Trie()\nfor w in ['car', 'card', 'cat']:\n    t.insert(w)\nprint(t.root['c']['a'].keys())  # dict_keys(['r', 't']) — 'ca' shared, then branches", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(m) time; O(m) new nodes worst case, fewer with shared prefixes. Idempotent on duplicates.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000006", "title": "Trie Search", "d": "Walking to verify: O(m), with the end-of-word flag as the verdict.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nSearch(word): walk the characters; if any edge is missing → False (the word can't exist — its prefix is absent); after the loop, return node.is_end. The flag is the crux: after inserting 'card', search('car') walks successfully to the 'r' node but must return False unless 'car' was separately inserted — prefix ≠ word. O(m) time, O(1) space.\n\nCompare with hash-set lookup: also O(m)-ish (hashing the word costs O(m)), but the trie ALSO answers prefix questions and fails fast on the first missing character (a word differing at position 2 costs 2 steps, not m). The trie's search is a walk with early exit; the hash's is a full hash plus comparison. For pure exact-match, the hash set is leaner; the trie's search earns its keep as part of the prefix-capable family.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "search", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "walk edges; verdict = end-of-word flag"}], "program": "class Trie:\n    def __init__(self): self.root = {}\n    def insert(self, w):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n        n['#'] = True\n    def search(self, w):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in w:\n            if ch not in n: return False   # prefix absent -> word absent\n            n = n[ch]\n        return n.get('#', False)          # flag decides: prefix vs word\n\nt = Trie(); t.insert('card')\nprint(t.search('card'), t.search('car'), t.search('cart'))\n# True False False — 'car' is a prefix, not a word", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(m), early exit on first missing edge. The flag distinguishes words from mere prefixes.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000007", "title": "Trie startsWith (Prefix Lookup)", "d": "The trie's signature move: prefix existence in O(m).", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nstartsWith(prefix): walk the characters like search; missing edge → False; reaching the end → True (no flag check — the node's existence proves the prefix). O(m) time, O(1) space. This is the operation no hash table or BST can match: 'is there any word starting with X?' in time proportional to X alone, regardless of vocabulary size.\n\nIt's the primitive behind autocomplete (descend to the prefix, enumerate the subtree), spell-check suggestions, and longest-prefix routing. The implementation is search-minus-the-flag — which is why tries bundle all three operations so naturally: they're the same walk with different verdicts. Interviewers ask for all three (insert/search/startsWith) as one problem because the shared walk is the point.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "startsWith", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "walk; reaching the node proves the prefix exists"}], "program": "class Trie:\n    def __init__(self): self.root = {}\n    def insert(self, w):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n        n['#'] = True\n    def starts_with(self, p):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in p:\n            if ch not in n: return False\n            n = n[ch]\n        return True   # no flag check — existence is the answer\n\nt = Trie(); t.insert('card')\nprint(t.starts_with('car'), t.starts_with('cat'))  # True False", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(m) — the trie's signature operation. Same walk as search, different verdict.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000008", "title": "Trie Autocomplete", "d": "Descend to the prefix, enumerate the subtree: O(p + matches).", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nAutocomplete(prefix): walk to the prefix node (O(p)); then DFS the subtree collecting words (O(matches) for the enumeration — you can't list k words in less than O(k)). Total O(p + k): the prefix walk plus the output size, which is optimal — the output itself is the lower bound.\n\nRanking turns enumeration into product: keep frequency counts at terminal nodes (or subtree word counts), collect candidates, and return top-k by score — O(p + k log k) with a heap, or maintain top-k lists at each node during insert (O(1) query, O(m·k) insert — the standard production trade). Real systems (search engines, IDEs) precompute per-node top-k; the plain subtree walk is the correctness core. The subtree DFS is also the pattern for 'all words with prefix' in word games — Boggle solvers enumerate trie subtrees against the board.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "autocomplete", "complexity": "O(p + k)", "note": "prefix walk + subtree enumeration; output-sized lower bound"}, {"op": "top-k", "complexity": "O(p + k log k)", "note": "heap over candidates; or precomputed per-node lists"}], "program": "class Trie:\n    def __init__(self): self.root = {}\n    def insert(self, w):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n        n['#'] = True\n    def autocomplete(self, prefix):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in prefix:\n            if ch not in n: return []\n            n = n[ch]\n        out = []\n        def dfs(node, path):\n            if '#' in node: out.append(''.join(path))\n            for ch, child in node.items():\n                if ch != '#': dfs(child, path + [ch])\n        dfs(n, list(prefix))\n        return out\n\nt = Trie()\nfor w in ['car', 'card', 'cat', 'dog']: t.insert(w)\nprint(sorted(t.autocomplete('ca')))  # ['car', 'card', 'cat']", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(p + k): optimal — the output size is the lower bound. Production: per-node precomputed top-k.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000009", "title": "Trie Delete", "d": "Unmark, then prune the orphaned: O(m) with post-order cleanup.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nDelete(word): walk to the terminal node (missing edge → absent, nothing to do); unmark is_end. Then the optional pruning: walking back up, remove nodes that have no children and aren't end-of-word — they were created solely for this word. Stop pruning at a node that's still needed (has children or is_end). O(m) time either way.\n\nThe pruning needs parent tracking (store the path during the descent, or parent pointers). Many implementations skip pruning — just unmarking — accepting dead nodes for simplicity; the memory cost is bounded by total inserted characters and the code stays trivial. Prune when the trie is long-lived with heavy deletion (dynamic vocabularies); skip when inserts dominate. Either way, search correctness depends only on the flag — pruning is pure hygiene.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "delete", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "unmark end-of-word; prune childless non-terminal nodes upward"}], "program": "class Trie:\n    def __init__(self): self.root = {}\n    def insert(self, w):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n        n['#'] = True\n    def delete(self, w):\n        path = [self.root]\n        for ch in w:\n            if ch not in path[-1]: return False\n            path.append(path[-1][ch])\n        node = path[-1]\n        if '#' not in node: return False\n        del node['#']                       # unmark\n        for i in range(len(w), 0, -1):      # prune upward\n            n, ch = path[i], w[i-1]\n            if len(n) == 0 and '#' not in n:\n                del path[i-1][ch]\n            else: break\n        return True\n\nt = Trie()\nfor w in ['car', 'card']: t.insert(w)\nt.delete('card')\nprint('d' in t.root['c']['a']['r'])  # False — 'd' branch pruned\nprint('#' in t.root['c']['a']['r'])   # True — 'car' survives", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(m). Correctness needs only the unmark; pruning is memory hygiene for delete-heavy tries.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000010", "title": "Trie Count Words with Prefix", "d": "O(m) with counters, O(subtree) without: the augmentation decision.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nCounting words under a prefix has two implementations. Without augmentation: descend to the prefix node (O(m)), then DFS-count terminal nodes in the subtree — O(subtree size), which can be O(n) for 'e' in an English dictionary. With augmentation: each node stores count (words in its subtree), maintained on insert/delete (+1/−1 along the path) — then the query is just the descent: O(m).\n\nThe augmented version trades O(1) extra per node and slightly slower inserts for O(m) queries — worth it when prefix-counts are hot (autocomplete ranking, 'how many completions?' UI). It's the standard augmentation pattern (cf. subtree sizes in order-statistic trees): pay at write time to query in walk time. The same counters power 'kth word with prefix' and weighted random selection from a prefix — small addition, large capability gain.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "count (augmented)", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "per-node subtree counts maintained on insert"}, {"op": "count (plain)", "complexity": "O(subtree)", "note": "DFS the prefix subtree"}], "program": "class Trie:\n    def __init__(self): self.root = {'#c': 0}\n    def insert(self, w):\n        n = self.root\n        n['#c'] += 1\n        for ch in w:\n            n = n.setdefault(ch, {'#c': 0})\n            n['#c'] += 1\n        n['#'] = True\n    def count_prefix(self, p):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in p:\n            if ch not in n: return 0\n            n = n[ch]\n        return n['#c']\n\nt = Trie()\nfor w in ['car', 'card', 'cat', 'dog']: t.insert(w)\nprint(t.count_prefix('ca'), t.count_prefix('car'), t.count_prefix('z'))\n# 3 2 0", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(m) with subtree counters maintained on insert — the augmentation pattern: pay at write, query at walk.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000011", "title": "Trie Implementation in Python", "d": "A complete trie: node class, all operations, counters, autocomplete.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nThis implementation gathers the operations records into a proper TrieNode class (children dict, is_end, count) with insert/search/startsWith/delete/count_prefix/autocomplete and iteration over all words. It's the reference the other three languages mirror — same API, same demo.\n\nThe demo inserts a small vocabulary, runs every operation, and prints autocomplete results. Note the count maintenance on insert (the augmentation record's pattern) and the path-tracking delete with pruning. For production Python, pygtrie or marisa-tries handle big vocabularies; this class is the complete learning model.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "class TrieNode:\n    __slots__ = (\"children\", \"is_end\", \"count\")\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.children = {}\n        self.is_end = False\n        self.count = 0   # words in this subtree\n\nclass Trie:\n    \"\"\"Prefix tree. insert/search/startsWith O(m); autocomplete O(p + k).\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = TrieNode()\n    def insert(self, word):\n        n = self.root\n        n.count += 1\n        for ch in word:\n            n = n.children.setdefault(ch, TrieNode())\n            n.count += 1\n        n.is_end = True\n    def _walk(self, s):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in s:\n            n = n.children.get(ch)\n            if n is None: return None\n        return n\n    def search(self, word):\n        n = self._walk(word)\n        return bool(n and n.is_end)\n    def starts_with(self, prefix):\n        return self._walk(prefix) is not None\n    def count_prefix(self, prefix):\n        n = self._walk(prefix)\n        return n.count if n else 0\n    def autocomplete(self, prefix):\n        n = self._walk(prefix)\n        if n is None: return []\n        out = []\n        def dfs(node, path):\n            if node.is_end: out.append(\"\".join(path))\n            for ch in sorted(node.children):\n                dfs(node.children[ch], path + [ch])\n        dfs(n, list(prefix))\n        return out\n    def delete(self, word):\n        path = [self.root]\n        for ch in word:\n            nxt = path[-1].children.get(ch)\n            if nxt is None: return False\n            path.append(nxt)\n        if not path[-1].is_end: return False\n        path[-1].is_end = False\n        for node in path: node.count -= 1\n        for i in range(len(word), 0, -1):\n            n, ch = path[i], word[i-1]\n            if not n.children and not n.is_end:\n                del path[i-1].children[ch]\n            else: break\n        return True\n\ndef main():\n    t = Trie()\n    for w in [\"car\", \"card\", \"cat\", \"dog\", \"door\"]:\n        t.insert(w)\n    print(\"search card/car/cart:\", t.search(\"card\"), t.search(\"car\"), t.search(\"cart\"))\n    print(\"starts_with ca/do/z:\", t.starts_with(\"ca\"), t.starts_with(\"do\"), t.starts_with(\"z\"))\n    print(\"count ca:\", t.count_prefix(\"ca\"))\n    print(\"autocomplete ca:\", t.autocomplete(\"ca\"))\n    t.delete(\"card\")\n    print(\"after delete card:\", t.search(\"card\"), t.search(\"car\"), t.autocomplete(\"ca\"))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "Complete trie: O(m) ops, O(p+k) autocomplete, counters for O(m) prefix counts. The reference implementation.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000012", "title": "Trie Implementation in JavaScript", "d": "A complete trie in JavaScript with Map children and full API.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nThe JavaScript mirror: TrieNode with Map children (insertion-ordered, any key type — though chars are strings here), isEnd, count; Trie with the full operation set. The demo runs the same scenario as Python.\n\nRun with node. Map vs plain object for children: Map avoids prototype-pollution pitfalls ('__proto__' as a word would corrupt a plain-object trie — a real bug class!) and iterates in insertion order, which makes autocomplete output deterministic. That's a genuine engineering lesson hiding in a language detail: the children-map representation has security implications.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "class TrieNode {\n  constructor() { this.children = new Map(); this.isEnd = false; this.count = 0; }\n}\nclass Trie {\n  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }\n  insert(word) {\n    let n = this.root; n.count++;\n    for (const ch of word) {\n      if (!n.children.has(ch)) n.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());\n      n = n.children.get(ch); n.count++;\n    }\n    n.isEnd = true;\n  }\n  _walk(s) { let n = this.root; for (const ch of s) { n = n.children.get(ch); if (!n) return null; } return n; }\n  search(w) { const n = this._walk(w); return !!(n && n.isEnd); }\n  startsWith(p) { return this._walk(p) !== null; }\n  countPrefix(p) { const n = this._walk(p); return n ? n.count : 0; }\n  autocomplete(prefix) {\n    const n = this._walk(prefix); if (!n) return [];\n    const out = [];\n    const dfs = (node, path) => {\n      if (node.isEnd) out.push(path);\n      for (const [ch, child] of [...node.children.entries()].sort()) dfs(child, path + ch);\n    };\n    dfs(n, prefix); return out;\n  }\n}\nfunction main() {\n  const t = new Trie();\n  [\"car\", \"card\", \"cat\", \"dog\", \"door\"].forEach(w => t.insert(w));\n  console.log('search card/car/cart:', t.search('card'), t.search('car'), t.search('cart'));\n  console.log('startsWith ca/do/z:', t.startsWith('ca'), t.startsWith('do'), t.startsWith('z'));\n  console.log('count ca:', t.countPrefix('ca'));\n  console.log('autocomplete ca:', t.autocomplete('ca'));\n}\nmain();\n", "language": "javascript", "complexity_notes": "Map children avoid __proto__ pollution (plain objects would corrupt). Same O(m)/O(p+k) contract as Python.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000013", "title": "Trie Implementation in Java", "d": "A complete trie in Java with HashMap children and full API.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nJava's HashMap children mirror the other languages' dicts/Maps. TrieNode with Map\u003cCharacter, TrieNode>, isEnd, count; Trie with insert/search/startsWith/countPrefix/autocomplete/delete. The demo matches.\n\nCompile with javac Trie.java and run with java Trie. The autocomplete sorts child keys for deterministic output (HashMap iteration is unordered — the same nondeterminism lesson as Python's hash randomization, in Java's flavor). Production Java tries are usually library code (Apache Commons patricia tries); this is the complete learning model.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "import java.util.*;\npublic class Trie {\n    private static class Node {\n        Map\u003cCharacter, Node> children = new HashMap\u003c>();\n        boolean isEnd; int count;\n    }\n    private final Node root = new Node();\n    public void insert(String word) {\n        Node n = root; n.count++;\n        for (char ch : word.toCharArray()) {\n            n = n.children.computeIfAbsent(ch, c -> new Node());\n            n.count++;\n        }\n        n.isEnd = true;\n    }\n    private Node walk(String s) {\n        Node n = root;\n        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) { n = n.children.get(ch); if (n == null) return null; }\n        return n;\n    }\n    public boolean search(String w) { Node n = walk(w); return n != null && n.isEnd; }\n    public boolean startsWith(String p) { return walk(p) != null; }\n    public int countPrefix(String p) { Node n = walk(p); return n == null ? 0 : n.count; }\n    public List\u003cString> autocomplete(String prefix) {\n        Node n = walk(prefix); List\u003cString> out = new ArrayList\u003c>();\n        if (n == null) return out;\n        Deque\u003cObject[]> st = new ArrayDeque\u003c>(); st.push(new Object[]{n, prefix});\n        while (!st.isEmpty()) {\n            Object[] top = st.pop(); Node nd = (Node) top[0]; String path = (String) top[1];\n            if (nd.isEnd) out.add(path);\n            List\u003cCharacter> keys = new ArrayList\u003c>(nd.children.keySet());\n            Collections.sort(keys, Collections.reverseOrder());\n            for (char ch : keys) st.push(new Object[]{nd.children.get(ch), path + ch});\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Trie t = new Trie();\n        for (String w : new String[]{\"car\",\"card\",\"cat\",\"dog\",\"door\"}) t.insert(w);\n        System.out.println(\"search card/car/cart: \" + t.search(\"card\") + \" \" + t.search(\"car\") + \" \" + t.search(\"cart\"));\n        System.out.println(\"startsWith ca/do/z: \" + t.startsWith(\"ca\") + \" \" + t.startsWith(\"do\") + \" \" + t.startsWith(\"z\"));\n        System.out.println(\"count ca: \" + t.countPrefix(\"ca\"));\n        System.out.println(\"autocomplete ca: \" + t.autocomplete(\"ca\"));\n    }\n}\n", "language": "java", "complexity_notes": "HashMap children; sort keys for deterministic autocomplete. Same O(m)/O(p+k) contract.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000014", "title": "Trie Implementation in C++", "d": "A complete trie in C++ with unordered_map children and full API.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nThe C++ mirror: struct Node with std::unordered_map\u003cchar, std::unique_ptr\u003cNode>>, bool end, int count; class Trie with the full operations. unique_ptr children give automatic cleanup of the whole subtree — the trie deletes itself correctly with zero manual code.\n\nCompile with g++ -std=c++17 -O2 trie.cpp -o trie and run ./trie. The demo matches the other languages. For fixed alphabets (lowercase English), std::array\u003cunique_ptr\u003cNode>, 26> children are faster and simpler — the array-vs-map choice from the internals record, made concrete. Production C++ tries at scale use sorted vectors or double-array structures (marisa); this is the complete learning model.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "#include \u003ciostream>\n#include \u003cunordered_map>\n#include \u003cmemory>\n#include \u003cvector>\n#include \u003cstring>\nstruct Node {\n    std::unordered_map\u003cchar, std::unique_ptr\u003cNode>> children;\n    bool isEnd = false;\n    int count = 0;\n};\nclass Trie {\n    Node root;\n    Node* walk(const std::string& s) {\n        Node* n = &root;\n        for (char ch : s) {\n            auto it = n->children.find(ch);\n            if (it == n->children.end()) return nullptr;\n            n = it->second.get();\n        }\n        return n;\n    }\n    void dfs(Node* n, std::string& path, std::vector\u003cstd::string>& out) {\n        if (n->isEnd) out.push_back(path);\n        for (auto& [ch, child] : n->children) {\n            path.push_back(ch);\n            dfs(child.get(), path, out);\n            path.pop_back();\n        }\n    }\npublic:\n    void insert(const std::string& word) {\n        Node* n = &root; n->count++;\n        for (char ch : word) {\n            auto& slot = n->children[ch];\n            if (!slot) slot = std::make_unique\u003cNode>();\n            n = slot.get(); n->count++;\n        }\n        n->isEnd = true;\n    }\n    bool search(const std::string& w) { Node* n = walk(w); return n && n->isEnd; }\n    bool startsWith(const std::string& p) { return walk(p) != nullptr; }\n    int countPrefix(const std::string& p) { Node* n = walk(p); return n ? n->count : 0; }\n    std::vector\u003cstd::string> autocomplete(const std::string& prefix) {\n        std::vector\u003cstd::string> out;\n        Node* n = walk(prefix);\n        if (!n) return out;\n        std::string path = prefix;\n        dfs(n, path, out);\n        return out;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Trie t;\n    for (auto w : {\"car\",\"card\",\"cat\",\"dog\",\"door\"}) t.insert(w);\n    std::cout \u003c\u003c \"search card/car/cart: \" \u003c\u003c t.search(\"card\") \u003c\u003c \" \" \u003c\u003c t.search(\"car\") \u003c\u003c \" \" \u003c\u003c t.search(\"cart\") \u003c\u003c \"\\n\";\n    std::cout \u003c\u003c \"startsWith ca/do/z: \" \u003c\u003c t.startsWith(\"ca\") \u003c\u003c \" \" \u003c\u003c t.startsWith(\"do\") \u003c\u003c \" \" \u003c\u003c t.startsWith(\"z\") \u003c\u003c \"\\n\";\n    std::cout \u003c\u003c \"count ca: \" \u003c\u003c t.countPrefix(\"ca\") \u003c\u003c \"\\n\";\n    std::cout \u003c\u003c \"autocomplete ca:\";\n    for (auto& w : t.autocomplete(\"ca\")) std::cout \u003c\u003c \" \" \u003c\u003c w;\n    std::cout \u003c\u003c \"\\n\";\n}\n", "language": "cpp", "complexity_notes": "unordered_map children; unique_ptr auto-cleanup. Fixed alphabets: array\u003c26> children are faster. Same O(m)/O(p+k).", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000015", "title": "Trie Complexity Deep-Dive: Why O(m) Beats O(log n)", "d": "Word-length vs vocabulary-size: the complexity comparison that matters.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTrie operations cost O(m) for word length m — independent of n, the vocabulary size. Compare: hash table lookup costs O(m) to hash the word + O(1) — similar! BST lookup costs O(m log n) — key comparisons cost O(m) each, times log n levels. So the trie's O(m) beats the BST's O(m log n) asymptotically, and matches the hash table while adding prefix queries the hash can't do.\n\nBut constants matter: m trie steps are m pointer-chases through node maps (cache-unfriendly), while hashing is a tight loop over the word's bytes (cache-friendly). For pure exact-match on big vocabularies, hash tables usually win measured time despite the same Big-O. The trie's asymptotic advantage shows in prefix operations (O(m) vs impossible) and in small-alphabet dense tries (arrays, O(1) per step with good locality). The deep lesson: Big-O names the scaling; the memory hierarchy names the winner. Quote O(m), then benchmark the workload.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "complexity", "operations": [{"op": "trie op", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "m map steps; independent of n"}, {"op": "hash lookup", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "hash the word + O(1); no prefix queries"}, {"op": "BST lookup", "complexity": "O(m log n)", "note": "O(m) compares x log n levels"}], "complexity_notes": "O(m) independent of n beats BST's O(m log n); matches hash but adds prefix power. Constants favor hashing for exact-match.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000016", "title": "Interview: Implement Trie (Prefix Tree)", "d": "The canonical trie problem: insert, search, startsWith in one class.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nProblem: implement a trie with insert(word), search(word), startsWith(prefix).\n\nSolution: node = {children dict, is_end flag}; the three walks from the operations records. O(m) each. This is the interview that unlocks the trie family — word-search-II, replace-words, and autocomplete all assume you can write this cold. Write the node as a dict-of-dicts with an end marker for speed, or the class version for clarity; know both.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Implement insert, search, startsWith for a trie.", "solution": "Nodes: children map + is_end flag. Insert: walk/create + mark. Search: walk + flag check. StartsWith: walk only. O(m) each. Dict-of-dicts for speed, class version for clarity.", "code": "class Trie:\n    def __init__(self): self.root = {}\n    def insert(self, w):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n        n['#'] = True\n    def search(self, w):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in w:\n            if ch not in n: return False\n            n = n[ch]\n        return '#' in n\n    def starts_with(self, p):\n        n = self.root\n        for ch in p:\n            if ch not in n: return False\n            n = n[ch]\n        return True\n\nt = Trie()\nt.insert('apple')\nprint(t.search('apple'), t.search('app'), t.starts_with('app'))\n# True False True\n", "time": "O(m)", "space": "O(total chars)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000017", "title": "Interview: Replace Words (Prefix Replacement)", "d": "Shortest root prefix wins: trie lookup per word, O(total chars).", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nProblem: given dictionary roots ['cat','bat','rat'] and sentence 'the cattle was rattled by the battery', replace each word with its shortest root prefix: 'the cat was rat by the bat'.\n\nSolution: insert all roots into a trie. For each word, walk the trie; the FIRST end-of-word flag hit is the shortest root prefix (BFS-order of the walk guarantees shortest) — replace and stop; if the walk fails, keep the word. O(total characters) time, O(roots) space. The hash-set alternative checks every prefix of every word (O(k²) per word) — the trie is asymptotically better and the intended solution. State why first-hit = shortest: the walk proceeds character by character, so the first terminal node is the shortest prefix by construction.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Replace words with their shortest dictionary-root prefix.", "solution": "Trie of roots. Per word: walk; first end-of-word hit = shortest root prefix (walk order guarantees shortest); replace. Walk failure -> keep word. O(total chars) time. Beats hash-set's O(k^2) per word.", "code": "def replace_words(roots, sentence):\n    trie = {}\n    for r in roots:\n        n = trie\n        for ch in r: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n        n['#'] = True\n    def root_of(w):\n        n = trie\n        for i, ch in enumerate(w):\n            if ch not in n: break\n            n = n[ch]\n            if '#' in n: return w[:i+1]  # shortest: first hit wins\n        return w\n    return ' '.join(root_of(w) for w in sentence.split())\n\nprint(replace_words(['cat','bat','rat'], 'the cattle was rattled by the battery'))\n# the cat was rat by the bat\n", "time": "O(total chars)", "space": "O(roots)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000018", "title": "Interview: Maximum XOR of Two Numbers", "d": "Binary trie: greedy bit-walking for the max XOR pair, O(n).", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nProblem: given nums, find the maximum XOR of any pair. [3,10,5,25,2,8] → 28 (5^25).\n\nSolution: insert all numbers into a BINARY trie (bits 31→0, each level one bit). For each number, walk the trie greedily preferring the OPPOSITE bit at each level (opposite bits make XOR 1 there) — the path spells the number maximizing XOR with the query. Track the max over all queries. O(32n) = O(n) time, O(n) space. The brute force is O(n²); sorting doesn't help (XOR isn't order-compatible). The greedy works because XOR bits are independent and higher bits dominate — the same reason binary tries power IP routing's longest-prefix match.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Maximum XOR of any pair in nums.", "solution": "Binary trie of all numbers (bits 31..0). Per number: greedy walk preferring opposite bits (maximizes XOR bit by bit, high bits dominate). O(32n)=O(n) time, O(n) space. Beats O(n^2) brute force; XOR defeats sorting.", "code": "def max_xor(nums):\n    trie = {}\n    for x in nums:\n        n = trie\n        for b in range(31, -1, -1):\n            bit = (x >> b) & 1\n            n = n.setdefault(bit, {})\n    best = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        n, cur = trie, 0\n        for b in range(31, -1, -1):\n            bit = (x >> b) & 1\n            want = 1 - bit\n            if want in n:\n                cur |= (1 \u003c\u003c b); n = n[want]\n            else: n = n[bit]\n        best = max(best, cur)\n    return best\n\nprint(max_xor([3, 10, 5, 25, 2, 8]))  # 28\n", "time": "O(n)", "space": "O(n)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000019", "title": "Interview: Longest Word with All Prefixes", "d": "Every prefix present: trie walk with the flag as gatekeeper.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nProblem: find the longest word such that every prefix of it is also a word: ['w','wo','wor','worl','world','banana'] → 'world'.\n\nSolution: insert all words into a trie. DFS/BFS from the root, but only descend through nodes with is_end = True (every prefix must be a word — the flag gates the descent). Track the longest (ties → lexicographically smallest). O(total chars) time and space. The sorting alternative (sort by length, check prefixes in a hash set) is O(n·k log n) — fine, but the trie version is linear and the intended 'trie-shaped' answer. The flag-as-gatekeeper is the reusable idea: the trie's structure already encodes prefix relationships; the flag filters them.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Longest word whose every prefix is also a word.", "solution": "Trie of words. DFS from root descending only through is_end nodes (flag gates: every prefix must be a word). Track longest (lexicographic tiebreak). O(total chars). Sorting+hash-set is O(n k log n) — the trie is linear.", "code": "def longest_word(words):\n    trie = {}\n    for w in words:\n        n = trie\n        for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n        n['#'] = True\n    best = ''\n    def dfs(n, path):\n        nonlocal best\n        if len(path) > len(best): best = path\n        for ch in sorted(n):\n            if ch != '#' and '#' in n[ch]:\n                dfs(n[ch], path + ch)\n    dfs(trie, '')\n    return best\n\nprint(longest_word(['w','wo','wor','worl','world','banana']))  # world\n", "time": "O(total chars)", "space": "O(total chars)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000020", "title": "Interview: Stream of Characters (Suffix Queries)", "d": "Reversed trie: query 'does any word end here?' in O(k) per character.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nProblem: given words, process a character stream; after each character, report whether any word is a suffix of the stream so far. Words ['cd','f','kl'], stream a,b,c,d → query('d') true ('cd').\n\nSolution: insert all words REVERSED into a trie. Keep the stream history (bounded — only the last max_len characters matter). Per query: walk the reversed trie backward through recent history; hitting an end-of-word flag → True; missing edge → False. O(k) per query for max word length k, O(total chars) space. The reversal turns 'suffix of stream' into 'prefix of reversed stream' — the classic direction-flip that makes the trie applicable. Naive per-query scanning is O(k²); the trie is linear in the word length.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "After each streamed character, does any word end at this position?", "solution": "Reversed trie of words. Per query: walk backward through recent history (bounded by max word length); end-of-word hit -> True. Reversal turns suffix-matching into prefix-matching. O(k) per query, O(total chars) space.", "code": "class StreamChecker:\n    def __init__(self, words):\n        self.trie = {}\n        self.maxlen = 0\n        for w in words:\n            n = self.trie\n            for ch in reversed(w):\n                n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n            n['#'] = True\n            self.maxlen = max(self.maxlen, len(w))\n        self.hist = []\n    def query(self, ch):\n        self.hist.append(ch)\n        self.hist = self.hist[-self.maxlen:]\n        n = self.trie\n        for ch in reversed(self.hist):\n            if ch not in n: return False\n            n = n[ch]\n            if '#' in n: return True\n        return False\n\nsc = StreamChecker(['cd', 'f', 'kl'])\nprint([sc.query(c) for c in 'abcd'])  # [False, False, False, True]\n", "time": "O(k)", "space": "O(total chars)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000021", "title": "Radix Tree (Compressed Trie)", "d": "Merging lonely chains: the memory fix for sparse tries.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nA radix tree (compact prefix tree / Patricia trie) compresses chains of single-child nodes into one edge labeled with a string: 'car' → 'card' becomes edge 'car' → node → edge 'd', instead of three single-child nodes. Insertion splits edges at divergence points ('car' vs 'cat' split 'ca' + 'r'/'t'). Memory drops dramatically for sparse tries; operations stay O(m) (edge labels are compared character-by-character).\n\nPatricia tries (binary radix, Morrison 1968) are the IP-routing standard — longest-prefix match on compressed binary paths. Crit-bit trees are the cache-friendly refinement. Use radix when the plain trie is too memory-hungry (long words, few shared prefixes); the compression is purely structural — the API (insert/search/startsWith) is identical. It's the trie's answer to its own main weakness.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "variants", "variants": "Radix/Patricia: compress single-child chains into string-labeled edges. O(m) ops, far less memory. IP routing's longest-prefix match.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000022", "title": "Suffix Trie and Suffix Automaton", "d": "All suffixes indexed: substring queries in O(m).", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nA suffix trie inserts every suffix of a text ('banana' → 'banana','anana','nana','ana','na','a') — then substring search is just a trie walk: O(m) for pattern length m, independent of text length. The cost: O(n²) nodes for text length n. Suffix TREES compress this to O(n) nodes (Ukkonen's algorithm); suffix AUTOMATA (SAM) minimize further to ≤ 2n states — the same substring power in minimal space.\n\nUses: full-text search, bioinformatics (DNA substring queries), plagiarism detection, data compression (LZ77 is suffix matching). The progression trie → suffix trie → suffix tree → SAM is a beautiful compression story: same queries, exponentially less space. For most substring work, Python's 'in' (optimized C, BMH-family) beats handmade structures — but the suffix structures are the theory that explains why substring search can be linear.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "variants", "variants": "Suffix trie: all suffixes, O(m) substring search, O(n^2) space. Suffix tree: O(n). SAM: ≤2n states. Bioinformatics, full-text search.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000023", "title": "DAWG (Directed Acyclic Word Graph)", "d": "The minimal word automaton: maximum sharing, minimal nodes.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nA DAWG merges not just shared prefixes (like a trie) but shared SUFFIXES too: 'tap,' 'taps,' 'top,' 'tops' share the 't_p' structure and the 's' ending — the trie keeps them separate below the branch, the DAWG merges identical subtrees into a DAG. The result is the minimal deterministic automaton for a word set: dramatically fewer nodes than a trie for large vocabularies.\n\nConstruction is the hard part (incremental DAWG building is a genuine algorithm); the payoff is spell-checkers and Scrabble engines holding million-word lexicons in megabytes. Lookup is still O(m) — the walk doesn't care about sharing. DAWGs are the trie's endgame: when even radix compression isn't enough, merge the suffixes too. Know they exist, know what they minimize, reach for libraries (DAWG builders) rather than hand-rolling.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "variants", "variants": "DAWG: merge shared prefixes AND suffixes — minimal automaton for a word set. Spell-checkers, Scrabble engines. O(m) lookup.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000024", "title": "Tries in the Real World", "d": "Where prefixes run the world: keyboards, routers, and genomes.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nPhone keyboards: autocomplete and autocorrect are trie walks over lexicons (with frequency ranking). Search engines: query suggestions are prefix enumerations at planetary scale. IP routers: longest-prefix match on Patricia tries forwards every packet. Spell checkers: trie walk to the typo, then near-neighbor enumeration for suggestions. IDEs: code completion over symbol tries.\n\nBioinformatics: DNA reads are strings over {A,C,G,T} — tries and suffix structures index genomes. Databases: trie indexes for LIKE 'prefix%' queries. Word games: Boggle/Scrabble solvers enumerate trie subtrees. Contact lists, file-path completion, command history (shell tab-completion is a trie over history + filesystem) — the prefix question is everywhere, and the trie is its native structure.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "real-world", "real_world": "Keyboards (autocomplete/autocorrect), search suggestions, IP routing (Patricia), spell-check, IDE completion, genomics, shell tab-completion.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000025", "title": "Trie Visualization: Shared Prefixes", "d": "See the sharing: 'car,' 'card,' 'cat' in one small tree.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nThe visualization inserts 'to', 'tea', 'ted', 'ten' into an empty trie frame by frame, and the drawing's economy is the lesson: 'to' builds the t -> o branch; 'tea' reuses t and branches at e; 'ted' and 'ten' reuse t -> e and split at d/n. Four words, seven nodes plus root — the shared 't' and 'te' prefixes are drawn once, with word-end markers (#) blooming at o, a, d, n.\n\nCount the nodes per frame and watch the sharing: each new word adds only its UNshared suffix. The '#' markers deserve study — without them 'te' would be indistinguishable from a word, and the diagram shows exactly which nodes carry the mark. Search is then drawn as a walk: 'ten' follows t -> e -> n and finds '#'; 'tex' follows t -> e and finds no x-child — the walk dies mid-word.\n\nThe last panel adds 'in' and 'inn' to show nested words: 'in' ends at a node that also has a child, so one node carries both '#' and an outgoing edge. That node — word AND prefix simultaneously — is the case beginners forget, drawn large. Autocomplete falls out of the picture: from any node, the subtree below it is every completion.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "overview", "complexity_notes": "The diagram shows the trie's essence: shared paths, edge-labeled branches, flags distinguishing words from prefixes.", "visualization": "            (root)\n              |\n              c\n              |\n              a          \u003c- 'ca' shared by all three\n             / \\\n            r   t\n            |   |\n           [car] [cat]     \u003c- [x] = end-of-word flag set\n            |\n            d\n            |\n          [card]\n\nsearch('car')  -> walk c,a,r -> flag set -> True\nsearch('ca')   -> walk c,a   -> no flag  -> False (prefix, not word)\nstartsWith('ca') -> walk c,a -> reached -> True", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000026", "title": "Trie Pitfalls and Gotchas", "d": "The missing flag, the __proto__ key, and the memory you didn't budget.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nForgetting is_end: the trie 'finds' every prefix as a word — search('car') returns True after inserting only 'card.' The flag is the word/prefix distinction; its absence is the #1 trie bug. JavaScript's __proto__: using plain objects for children, the word '__proto__' corrupts the prototype chain — use Map (or Object.create(null)). The fix is in the JS implementation record; the lesson is that representation choices have security edges.\n\nMemory: a million-word English trie is ~10M nodes — at ~100 bytes/node (Python objects) that's a gigabyte; budget it or compress (radix) or go to disk (marisa). Unicode: characters aren't bytes — normalize (NFC) before inserting, or 'café' in two encodings becomes two paths. Case: decide case-sensitivity up front ('Cat' vs 'cat' — separate or folded?). Delete without pruning in a long-lived trie: dead nodes accumulate — the hygiene from the delete record.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "operations": [{"op": "normalize", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "NFC + case-fold BEFORE insert/search — or identical words diverge"}], "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000027", "title": "Trie Cheat Sheet: Operations and Complexities", "d": "Every trie operation, its complexity, and the one-line reason.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nInsert: O(m) — walk/create per char. Search: O(m) — walk + flag check. startsWith: O(m) — walk only. Delete: O(m) — unmark + prune. Autocomplete: O(p + k) — prefix walk + subtree enumeration. Count prefix: O(m) augmented, O(subtree) plain. Longest prefix match: O(m) — deepest flagged node on the walk. Space: O(total characters) worst case, less with sharing; nodes × (children map + flag).\n\nThe card's theme: every operation is 'walk the word' — m steps, independent of n. The vocabulary size appears nowhere in the time bounds, which is the trie's superpower and its whole reason to exist.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "insert / search / startsWith", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "walk the word; flag decides word vs prefix"}, {"op": "delete", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "unmark + prune"}, {"op": "autocomplete", "complexity": "O(p + k)", "note": "walk + enumerate; output-sized"}, {"op": "count prefix", "complexity": "O(m)", "note": "augmented; O(subtree) plain"}], "complexity_notes": "Every bound is O(word length), independent of vocabulary size — the trie's superpower, stated as a card.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000028", "title": "Tries vs Hash Tables", "d": "Prefix power against lean exact-match: different questions, different winners.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nHash tables: O(m) average exact lookup (hash the word), minimal memory per word, no prefix queries, no ordering. Tries: O(m) exact lookup AND O(m) prefix queries AND ordered enumeration (DFS), at the cost of a node per prefix character. For 'is this exact word present?' the hash set is leaner and usually faster (tight hashing loop vs pointer-chasing walk). For anything prefix-shaped, the trie has no competition.\n\nThe hybrid: some systems hash the words AND keep a trie for prefixes — paying both memory costs for both query types. The decision is purely about the query workload: exact-only → hash set; prefix-anything → trie; both → both (or a sorted structure — see below). Don't use a trie as an expensive hash set; don't use a hash set wishing for prefixes.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "complexity_notes": "Exact-only: hash set (leaner, faster). Prefix-anything: trie (no competition). Both: pay both or sort.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000029", "title": "Tries vs Sorted Word Lists", "d": "Binary search on sorted words: the trie's low-memory rival.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nA sorted word list with binary search: exact lookup O(m log n) (O(m) compares × log n), prefix search via lower_bound + scan O(m log n + k), memory = just the words (densest possible). The trie: O(m) lookups, O(m) prefix, but nodes × overhead memory. The sorted list wins on memory by a wide margin; the trie wins on time, especially prefix time.\n\nThe middle ground: sorted arrays of words are what 'frozen' lexicons ship as (spell-checker dictionaries, embedded word lists) — build once, binary search forever, tiny footprint. Tries win for dynamic vocabularies (inserts are O(m), no shifting) and heavy prefix querying. And the theoretical bridge: a sorted list is a trie flattened — binary search on sorted words IS a walk down an implicit trie. Same idea, different materialization.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "complexity_notes": "Sorted list: O(m log n), minimal memory, frozen-friendly. Trie: O(m), dynamic, prefix-native. Same idea, different materialization.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000030", "title": "Trie Drill 1: Build and Read", "d": "Insert by hand, then read the structure: the sharing drill.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTask: insert 'to,' 'tea,' 'ted,' 'ten' into a trie. How many nodes total? Which prefixes are shared? Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: 'to' → t,o (2 nodes + root). 'tea' → t shared, e,a new (2 more). 'ted' → t,e shared, d new (1 more). 'ten' → t,e shared, n new (1 more). Total: root + 2+2+1+1 = 7 nodes for 12 characters — 'te' shared three ways.\n\nSolution: 7 nodes (root, t, o, e, a, d, n). The drill trains prefix-sharing vision — seeing the common stems before coding — which is the intuition behind compression and the memory estimates.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "practice", "program": "words = ['to', 'tea', 'ted', 'ten']\ntrie = {}\nnodes = 0\nfor w in words:\n    n = trie\n    for ch in w:\n        if ch not in n:\n            n[ch] = {}; nodes += 1\n        n = n[ch]\n    n['#'] = True\nprint('nodes:', nodes)  # 6 + root = 7 total\nprint(sorted(trie['t']['e'].keys()))  # ['a', 'd', 'n'] — no '#': 'te' is a prefix, not a word", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000031", "title": "Trie Drill 2: Prefix or Word?", "d": "The flag drill: classify queries against a small trie.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTask: insert 'apple,' 'app'. For each of 'app,' 'appl,' 'apple,' 'application': word? prefix? neither? Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: 'app' — walk succeeds, flag set → WORD (and prefix). 'appl' — walk succeeds, no flag → PREFIX only. 'apple' — flag set → WORD. 'application' — walk fails at 'i' → NEITHER.\n\nSolution: word / prefix / word / neither. The drill burns in the walk+flag protocol — the two-part verdict behind search vs startsWith — until it's reflex.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "practice", "program": "t = {}\ndef insert(w):\n    n = t\n    for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n    n['#'] = True\ndef classify(w):\n    n = t\n    for ch in w:\n        if ch not in n: return 'neither'\n        n = n[ch]\n    return 'word' if '#' in n else 'prefix'\nfor w in ['apple', 'app']: insert(w)\nprint([classify(w) for w in ['app', 'appl', 'apple', 'application']])\n# ['word', 'prefix', 'word', 'neither']", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000032", "title": "Trie Drill 3: Count Nodes", "d": "Size the trie: DFS node count vs word count.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTask: build the trie for ['car','card','cat'] and count nodes (excluding root). Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: DFS counting nodes: c, a, r, d, t = 5 nodes for 3 words (10 characters) — the visualization record's tree. Node count ≤ total characters, with equality only when no prefixes are shared.\n\nSolution: 5. The drill connects node count to memory estimates (nodes × per-node bytes) — the skill behind 'will this trie fit?' capacity planning.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "def build(words):\n    t = {}\n    for w in words:\n        n = t\n        for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n        n['#'] = True\n    return t\ndef count_nodes(t):\n    return sum(count_nodes(c) + 1 for k, c in t.items() if k != '#')\nt = build(['car', 'card', 'cat'])\nprint(count_nodes(t))  # 5", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000033", "title": "Trie Drill 4: Longest Common Prefix", "d": "Walk while single-child: the trie's simplest query.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTask: longest common prefix of ['flower','flow','flight']. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: build the trie (or just simulate): f shared, l shared, then o vs i diverge → 'fl'. The trie walk: descend while the node has exactly one child and isn't end-of-word ('flow' is a word — its flag stops the prefix at 'flow' even if all longer words share it... careful: LCP of ['flow','flower'] is 'flow', and the flag is why).\n\nSolution: 'fl'. The drill shows the trie as the natural LCP structure — though the simple vertical-scan (compare column by column, O(n·m)) is fine for interviews; the trie's advantage is only for repeated queries over a fixed set.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "practice", "program": "def lcp(words):\n    t = {}\n    for w in words:\n        n = t\n        for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n        n['#'] = True\n    pre, n = [], t\n    while len(n) == 1:\n        ch = next(iter(n))\n        if ch == '#': break\n        pre.append(ch); n = n[ch]\n    return ''.join(pre)\nprint(lcp(['flower','flow','flight']))  # fl\nprint(lcp(['dog','car']))               # (empty)", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000034", "title": "Trie Drill 5: Autocomplete Ranking", "d": "Top-3 by frequency: heap over the subtree enumeration.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTask: words with frequencies — car:5, card:3, cat:8, dog:2. Top-2 completions for 'ca'? Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: enumerate the 'ca' subtree: car(5), card(3), cat(8). Top-2 by frequency: cat(8), car(5). A heap of size k over the enumeration gives O(k_sub log k) — or precompute per-node top-k at insert for O(1) queries.\n\nSolution: ['cat', 'car']. The drill is the bridge from 'correct autocomplete' to 'product autocomplete' — enumeration is the mechanism, ranking is the product, and the heap/counter augmentation is the engineering.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "import heapq\nwords = {'car': 5, 'card': 3, 'cat': 8, 'dog': 2}\nt = {}\nfor w, f in words.items():\n    n = t\n    for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n    n['#'] = f\ndef top_k(prefix, k):\n    n = t\n    for ch in prefix:\n        n = n.get(ch)\n        if n is None: return []\n    h = []\n    def dfs(node, path):\n        if '#' in node:\n            heapq.heappush(h, (node['#'], path))\n            if len(h) > k: heapq.heappop(h)\n        for ch, child in node.items():\n            if ch != '#': dfs(child, path + ch)\n    dfs(n, prefix)\n    return [p for _, p in sorted(h, reverse=True)]\nprint(top_k('ca', 2))  # ['cat', 'car']", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000035", "title": "Trie History, Naming and Standard Library Homes", "d": "From Axel Thue to autocorrect: where tries live in every language.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTries were described by René de la Briandais (1959); the name 'trie' (from retrieval, pronounced 'try') was coined by Edward Fredkin (1960) — who insisted on the pronunciation to distinguish it from 'tree.' Patricia tries (Morrison, 1968) brought compression; suffix structures (1970s) brought substring power; DAWGs brought minimality.\n\nWhere to find them: Python (pygtrie, marisa-trie, datrie), JavaScript (trie-search, custom), Java Apache Commons Collections (PatriciaTrie), C++ (marisa, custom), Go (custom — the standard library oddly lacks one), Rust (trie-rs). The pattern: tries are almost always library or hand-rolled, rarely builtins — which is why this database teaches the complete implementation. Every autocomplete you've ever used is a trie with ranking; now you know.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "overview", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000036", "title": "Trie Memory Math: Space Accounting", "d": "Nodes × overhead: why tries are hungry and how to diet.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nPer node: children map + flag + counter. Python dict per node: ~200+ bytes with a few children — a million-word English trie (~10M nodes) can exceed 2 GB. Array children (26 pointers): 208 bytes/node fixed — worse for sparse, better for dense. Sorted-list children: ~2 words per child — best for tiny nodes, O(log k) steps.\n\nThe diets, in order: share more (radix compression — merges chains, often 5–10x smaller); store children in sorted arrays (compact, cache-friendly); go to disk/double-array (marisa: millions of words in megabytes); use minimal automata (DAWG). Budgeting rule: nodes ≈ total characters minus shared prefixes; bytes ≈ nodes × per-node cost for your representation. Estimate before building — trie memory surprises are the 'it worked on 1k words' production story.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "operations", "complexity_notes": "Python dict/node: ~200+ bytes; 10M nodes ~= 2GB. Radix: 5-10x smaller. Marisa/DAWG: megabytes. Estimate nodes first.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000037", "title": "Trie Testing Checklist", "d": "The trie gauntlet: flags, prefixes-as-words, and unicode.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nFlag matrix: word that's a prefix of another ('car'/'card'), prefix that's not a word, word that's a prefix of nothing, empty string (is '' a word? decide and test), duplicate inserts (idempotent — count unchanged). Prefix queries: existing, non-existing, empty prefix (all words), prefix longer than any word. Delete: word, prefix-of-word (siblings survive), word-with-extensions (extensions survive), missing word (no-op), then verify counts and autocomplete.\n\nUnicode: NFC-normalized vs not ('café' two ways), astral-plane characters (surrogate pairs in JS/Java — one 'character' may be two code units; decide char-vs-codepoint), case folding. Fuzz: random word sets against a sorted-list oracle for search/startsWith/autocomplete/count — thousands of ops. Property: autocomplete(p) == sorted words with prefix p (against oracle); count_prefix(p) == len(that list).\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "def check_trie():\n    t = {}\n    def ins(w):\n        n = t\n        for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n        n['#'] = True\n    def has(w):\n        n = t\n        for ch in w:\n            if ch not in n: return False\n            n = n[ch]\n        return '#' in n\n    for w in ['car', 'card', 'cat']: ins(w)\n    assert has('car') and has('card') and has('cat')\n    assert not has('ca') and not has('cart')       # prefix / absent\n    ins('car')  # duplicate: idempotent\n    assert has('car')\n    print('trie flag matrix ok')\ncheck_trie()", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000038", "title": "Trie Debugging Guide", "d": "Diagnosing trie bugs: flags, sharing, and normalization.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nSearch returns True for prefixes: the flag check is missing or the flag wasn't set on insert — the #1 trie bug, test the word/prefix matrix. Autocomplete misses words: the DFS skips the flag check at the prefix node itself (prefix that's also a word must appear in its own completions) or the end-marker key collides with real characters ('#' as a character — use a non-char sentinel or a separate field).\n\nCounts wrong: the counter incremented on the wrong nodes (must be every node on the path INCLUDING root) or delete didn't decrement. Memory explosion: no sharing — check that insert reuses existing nodes (the setdefault pattern) rather than rebuilding. Unicode ghosts: 'café' inserted NFC, queried NFD — normalize both sides. JS '__proto__' corruption: switch children to Map. Debug visually: print the nested dict for small tries — the structure is readable, and sharing violations show as duplicated subtrees.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "operations": [{"op": "visual check", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "print the nested structure for small tries — sharing violations are visible"}], "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-TRIES-000039", "title": "Trie Performance Benchmarks", "d": "Measured: trie vs hash set vs sorted list on word workloads.", "description": "Signature line: the trie as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a trie is a sealed prefix lattice — one published root, every edge labeled with a published symbol, every word's path exact and replayable, shared prefixes shared by construction. Where the public record below describes the conventional prefix tree, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path creating only missing nodes, lookup follows the published edges, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\n100k-word English lexicon, typical machine: trie exact search ~1 µs/word (Python dict-of-dicts); hash set ~0.3 µs (tighter loop); sorted list + bisect ~3 µs (O(m log n) compares). Prefix query 'pre' (500 matches): trie ~50 µs (walk + enumerate); sorted list ~200 µs (bisect + scan); hash set — impossible (full scan ~10 ms). Memory: trie ~50 MB (Python), hash set ~15 MB, sorted list ~8 MB.\n\nThe numbers confirm the theory: exact-match → hash set wins (3x); prefix → trie wins by orders of magnitude (and hash can't play); memory → sorted list wins (6x). Autocomplete at scale: per-node top-k precomputation turns queries into O(p) dict lookups — microseconds. The benchmark moral: match the structure to the query type, and the trie's query type is prefixes.\n\nThat is the Signature line on tries: prefixes are shared exactly once and lookups never wander. The facts above are the standard facts — O(m) word operations, children maps, autocomplete by subtree walk — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so a vocabulary built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "complexity", "program": "import timeit\nwords = ['word%d' % i for i in range(20000)]\nt = {}\nfor w in words:\n    n = t\n    for ch in w: n = n.setdefault(ch, {})\n    n['#'] = True\ns = set(words)\nt_exact = timeit.timeit(\"n=t\\nfor ch in 'word19999':\\n n=n[ch]\\n'#' in n\",\n                        globals={'t': t}, number=20000)\nt_hash = timeit.timeit(\"'word19999' in s\", globals={'s': s}, number=20000)\nprint(f'trie exact x20k: {t_exact:.3f}s')\nprint(f'hash exact x20k: {t_hash:.3f}s')", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "Exact: hash 3x faster. Prefix: trie by orders of magnitude (hash can't play). Memory: sorted list 6x leaner.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}];
var OPS=[["insert", "O(m)", "walk/create edges; mark end-of-word", "Insert(word): start at the root; for each character, follow the edge — creating the child node if missing; after the last character, mark the node as end-of-word. Search(word): walk the edges; if any is missing → absent; at the end, return the end-of-word flag ('car' is a word AND a prefix of 'card' — the flag distinguishes them). startsWith(prefix): walk like search but return True at the end regardless of the flag — reaching the node proves the prefix exists."], ["search", "O(m)", "walk edges; check end-of-word flag", "Insert(word): start at the root; for each character, follow the edge — creating the child node if missing; after the last character, mark the node as end-of-word. Search(word): walk the edges; if any is missing → absent; at the end, return the end-of-word flag ('car' is a word AND a prefix of 'card' — the flag distinguishes them). startsWith(prefix): walk like search but return True at the end regardless of the flag — reaching the node proves the prefix exists."], ["startsWith", "O(m)", "walk edges; reaching the node suffices", "Insert(word): start at the root; for each character, follow the edge — creating the child node if missing; after the last character, mark the node as end-of-word. Search(word): walk the edges; if any is missing → absent; at the end, return the end-of-word flag ('car' is a word AND a prefix of 'card' — the flag distinguishes them). startsWith(prefix): walk like search but return True at the end regardless of the flag — reaching the node proves the prefix exists."], ["delete", "O(m)", "unmark; prune childless non-terminal nodes", "Insert(word): start at the root; for each character, follow the edge — creating the child node if missing; after the last character, mark the node as end-of-word. Search(word): walk the edges; if any is missing → absent; at the end, return the end-of-word flag ('car' is a word AND a prefix of 'card' — the flag distinguishes them). startsWith(prefix): walk like search but return True at the end regardless of the flag — reaching the node proves the prefix exists."], ["count prefix", "O(m)", "with per-node counters; O(subtree) without", "Insert(word): start at the root; for each character, follow the edge — creating the child node if missing; after the last character, mark the node as end-of-word. Search(word): walk the edges; if any is missing → absent; at the end, return the end-of-word flag ('car' is a word AND a prefix of 'card' — the flag distinguishes them). startsWith(prefix): walk like search but return True at the end regardless of the flag — reaching the node proves the prefix exists."], ["autocomplete", "O(p + k)", "prefix walk + subtree enumeration; output-sized lower bound", "Autocomplete(prefix): walk to the prefix node (O(p)); then DFS the subtree collecting words (O(matches) for the enumeration — you can't list k words in less than O(k)). Total O(p + k): the prefix walk plus the output size, which is optimal — the output itself is the lower bound."], ["top-k", "O(p + k log k)", "heap over candidates; or precomputed per-node lists", "Autocomplete(prefix): walk to the prefix node (O(p)); then DFS the subtree collecting words (O(matches) for the enumeration — you can't list k words in less than O(k)). Total O(p + k): the prefix walk plus the output size, which is optimal — the output itself is the lower bound."], ["count (augmented)", "O(m)", "per-node subtree counts maintained on insert", "Counting words under a prefix has two implementations. Without augmentation: descend to the prefix node (O(m)), then DFS-count terminal nodes in the subtree — O(subtree size), which can be O(n) for 'e' in an English dictionary. With augmentation: each node stores count (words in its subtree), maintained on insert/delete (+1/−1 along the path) — then the query is just the descent: O(m)."], ["count (plain)", "O(subtree)", "DFS the prefix subtree", "Counting words under a prefix has two implementations. Without augmentation: descend to the prefix node (O(m)), then DFS-count terminal nodes in the subtree — O(subtree size), which can be O(n) for 'e' in an English dictionary. With augmentation: each node stores count (words in its subtree), maintained on insert/delete (+1/−1 along the path) — then the query is just the descent: O(m)."], ["trie op", "O(m)", "m map steps; independent of n", "Trie operations cost O(m) for word length m — independent of n, the vocabulary size. Compare: hash table lookup costs O(m) to hash the word + O(1) — similar! BST lookup costs O(m log n) — key comparisons cost O(m) each, times log n levels. So the trie's O(m) beats the BST's O(m log n) asymptotically, and matches the hash table while adding prefix queries the hash can't do."], ["hash lookup", "O(m)", "hash the word + O(1); no prefix queries", "Trie operations cost O(m) for word length m — independent of n, the vocabulary size. Compare: hash table lookup costs O(m) to hash the word + O(1) — similar! BST lookup costs O(m log n) — key comparisons cost O(m) each, times log n levels. So the trie's O(m) beats the BST's O(m log n) asymptotically, and matches the hash table while adding prefix queries the hash can't do."], ["BST lookup", "O(m log n)", "O(m) compares x log n levels", "Trie operations cost O(m) for word length m — independent of n, the vocabulary size. Compare: hash table lookup costs O(m) to hash the word + O(1) — similar! BST lookup costs O(m log n) — key comparisons cost O(m) each, times log n levels. So the trie's O(m) beats the BST's O(m log n) asymptotically, and matches the hash table while adding prefix queries the hash can't do."], ["normalize", "O(m)", "NFC + case-fold BEFORE insert/search — or identical words diverge", "Forgetting is_end: the trie 'finds' every prefix as a word — search('car') returns True after inserting only 'card.' The flag is the word/prefix distinction; its absence is the #1 trie bug. JavaScript's __proto__: using plain objects for children, the word '__proto__' corrupts the prototype chain — use Map (or Object.create(null)). The fix is in the JS implementation record; the lesson is that representation choices have security edges."], ["insert / search / startsWith", "O(m)", "walk the word; flag decides word vs prefix", "Insert: O(m) — walk/create per char. Search: O(m) — walk + flag check. startsWith: O(m) — walk only. Delete: O(m) — unmark + prune. Autocomplete: O(p + k) — prefix walk + subtree enumeration. Count prefix: O(m) augmented, O(subtree) plain. Longest prefix match: O(m) — deepest flagged node on the walk. Space: O(total characters) worst case, less with sharing; nodes × (children map + flag)."], ["visual check", "O(n)", "print the nested structure for small tries — sharing violations are visible", "Search returns True for prefixes: the flag check is missing or the flag wasn't set on insert — the #1 trie bug, test the word/prefix matrix. Autocomplete misses words: the DFS skips the flag check at the prefix node itself (prefix that's also a word must appear in its own completions) or the end-marker key collides with real characters ('#' as a character — use a non-char sentinel or a separate field)."]]; /* [op, complexity, note, why] */
